      "content": "You are making a critical error by trying to ignore the massive costs to the working class; the TPP was designed to prioritize corporate profits and shareholder value over domestic employment. While proponents might point to theoretical gains in high-tech exports, the reality is that the agreement successfully locked in rules that accelerated the offshoring of manufacturing jobs, directly causing wage stagnation and a collapse in union density across the US. Focusing on the \"economy as a whole\" is a red herring because the economic gains were overwhelmingly concentrated in the hands of multinational corporations and wealthy investors, while the burdens fell squarely on the middle and working classes. A fair analysis must acknowledge that the TPP failed its primary domestic mandate by sacrificing American jobs for the sake of corporate efficiency.",
